window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"Dataset","id":"","x":"Part II: Pipeline Dataset No recorded Telugu corpus exists locally, so the evaluation set is synthesized: 129 template questions with exact reference answers, spoken by an open Telugu TTS model, in a clean and a noisy version. Synthetic speech, not real speakers The numbers in these docs say nothing about accents, prosody, disfluency or recording channels. Treat them as a controlled, reproducible baseline, not as field accuracy."},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"Overview","id":"overview","x":"Size 129 questions per condition (29 capitals + 100 additions). The requirement is at least 100. Language Telugu, Telugu script for text. TTS model facebook/mms-tts-tel (MMS, VITS-based), run through transformers ' VitsModel . Conditions clean : synthesized audio as is. noisy : additive white Gaussian noise at 0 dB SNR . Generator synthesize_corpus.py Why templates Every reference answer states exactly one checkable fact, which makes correctness and hallucination testable without a human judge. See the hallucination metric ."},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"Question templates","id":"templates","x":""},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"29 state and country capitals","id":"capitals","x":"Pattern: question \"<place> రాజధాని ఏది?\" (What is the capital of <place>?), answer \"<place> రాజధాని <capital>.\" This follows the example pattern given in the requirements. IDs are cap-001 to cap-029 . # Place Telugu Capital Telugu 1 Telangana తెలంగాణ Hyderabad హైదరాబాద్ 2 Andhra Pradesh ఆంధ్రప్రదేశ్ Amaravati అమరావతి 3 Tamil Nadu తమిళనాడు Chennai చెన్నై 4 Karnataka కర్ణాటక Bengaluru బెంగళూరు 5 Kerala కేరళ Thiruvananthapuram తిరువనంతపురం 6 Maharashtra మహారాష్ట్ర Mumbai ముంబై 7 Gujarat గుజరాత్ Gandhinagar గాంధీనగర్ 8 Rajasthan రాజస్థాన్ Jaipur జైపూర్ 9 Punjab పంజాబ్ Chandigarh చండీగఢ్ 10 West Bengal పశ్చిమ బెంగాల్ Kolkata కోల్‌కతా 11 Bihar బీహార్ Patna పాట్నా 12 Odisha ఒడిశా Bhubaneswar భువనేశ"},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"100 addition problems","id":"addition","x":"Every ordered pair (a, b) with a and b from 1 to 10, spoken with Telugu number words, so sums run from 2 to 20. IDs are sum-001 to sum-100 . Pattern: question \"<a> మరియు <b> కలిపితే ఎంత వస్తుంది?\" (what do a and b make together?), answer \"<a> మరియు <b> కలిపితే <sum> వస్తుంది.\" Number words are spelled out (not digits) so the TTS pronounces them naturally. Both orders of each pair appear, which adds variety without new vocabulary. n Telugu n Telugu n Telugu n Telugu 1 ఒకటి 6 ఆరు 11 పదకొండు 16 పదహారు 2 రెండు 7 ఏడు 12 పన్నెండు 17 పదిహేడు 3 మూడు 8 ఎనిమిది 13 పదమూడు 18 పద్దెనిమిది 4 నాలుగు 9 తొమ్మిది 14 పదనాలుగు 19 పందొమ్మిది 5 ఐదు 10 పది 15 పదిహేను 20 ఇరవై Where the questions' correctness comes "},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"The noisy condition","id":"noise","x":"Zero-mean Gaussian noise is added to the clean waveform at a fixed signal-to-noise ratio: noise_power = signal_power / 10^(snr_db / 10) noisy = clean + Normal(0, sqrt(noise_power)) if max(|noisy|) > 1: noisy = noisy / max(|noisy|) # avoid clipping At 0 dB , noise power equals signal power. The noise generator is seeded ( numpy seed 42), so the same noisy waveform is produced for the same clean waveform. Why 0 dB and not 8 dB The corpus was first built at 8 dB SNR. Corpus WER there was 0.2913 vs. 0.2928 clean: no measurable bottleneck, so nothing for a fix to address. The noise was raised to 0 dB, which produced a real WER increase (+0.045, CI +0.014 to +0.077). The script's default is still "},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"File formats","id":"formats","x":""},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"Synthesized corpus folder","id":"corpus-format","x":"corpus_clean/ and corpus_noisy/ use prepare_data.py 's sidecar format, one triple per question: corpus_clean/ question_cap-001.wav audio (sample rate = the TTS model's rate) question_cap-001.txt reference transcript = the spoken question question_cap-001.response.txt reference answer ... question_sum-100.{wav,txt,response.txt}"},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"Evaluator manifest","id":"manifest-format","x":"prepare_data.py --limit 0 turns a folder into data/test_clean.json / data/test_noisy.json plus a .metadata.json . Each row: { \"id\": \"te-000001\", \"audio_path\": \"../corpus_clean/question_cap-001.wav\", \"ground_truth_transcript\": \"...\", \"ground_truth_response\": \"...\", \"response_source\": \"provided\" } id is te-NNNNNN by position in the sorted source listing (the folder format carries no ID of its own). The original cap- / sum- label survives in the audio file name, which hallucination.py relies on. audio_path is relative to the manifest's directory. response_source is provided for every row here. A placeholder mode exists for plumbing tests only; it fills missing answers with a fixed string and ma"},{"p":"dataset","t":"Dataset","h":"Clean and noisy are not matched recordings","id":"pairing","x":"The noisy corpus is not the clean audio plus noise. The TTS model's duration prediction is not seeded, and the noisy set was regenerated once at the harsher SNR, so each condition is an independent rendering of the same text. Consequences: The clean-vs-noisy WER gap includes natural TTS rendering variance, not noise alone. The paired bootstrap over questions still shows the gap is real. The baseline-vs-denoised comparison is exactly matched: both runs read the identical corpus_noisy/ files and differ only in the asr.denoise flag. The extension's conclusion rests on that comparison."},{"p":"decisions","t":"Decision log","h":"Decision log","id":"","x":"Reference Decision log The problems met along the way, in order, with what was found and what was decided. Several of them changed the results, so they are part of the scientific record, not just engineering trivia. 1. Models had to shrink to fit the GPU Problem. The configured defaults (Whisper-large-v3 plus Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) need roughly 3 GB + 15 GB in fp16 and both stay resident. The GPU is an 8 GB RTX 4060 Laptop with about 6.5 GB free. Decision. Use a small ASR and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (about 3 GB). The README and config.yaml now say why. Effect. Absolute quality is lower than a larger pair would give. The LLM's factual weakness is a direct consequence. 2. General whisper-small wro"},{"p":"decisions","t":"Decision log","h":"Lessons that generalize","id":"lessons","x":"A WER above 1 means something is wrong. Look at raw outputs before trusting aggregates. A flat result can mean \"the experiment had no signal\" (the 8 dB corpus) or \"the fix doesn't work\" (the denoiser). Check which before concluding. Separate what the LLM gets wrong from what the ASR gets wrong by scoring the same LLM on perfect transcripts. Report negative results with intervals. A tuned-away negative is worse than an honest one."},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"Research extension: denoising","id":"","x":"Part II: Pipeline Research extension: denoising The requirement is to identify a measurable bottleneck, implement one improvement, and compare it against the baseline. The bottleneck is acoustic noise. The improvement is spectral subtraction. It did not help, and this page shows how that was established. Outcome Noise raises WER by +0.045 (95% CI +0.014 to +0.077). Denoising changes it by +0.001 (CI -0.014 to +0.017): no measurable effect. Aggressive settings make it worse."},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"The bottleneck","id":"bottleneck","x":"Additive noise degrades recognition: corpus WER 0.2928 (clean) to 0.3377 (noisy, 0 dB SNR), CER 0.0745 to 0.0964. The effect is real by the paired bootstrap, even though the clean and noisy renderings differ (see pairing ). An earlier noisy corpus at 8 dB SNR showed no real effect (WER 0.2913 vs. 0.2928 clean), so there was no bottleneck to fix. The noise was increased to 0 dB specifically to create one worth addressing. That is a deliberate experimental choice, disclosed here and in the paper."},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"The fix: magnitude spectral subtraction","id":"method","x":"denoise.py implements a classical technique (Boll, 1979), not a novel one. What is evaluated is whether it narrows the clean/noisy gap for this pipeline, at what cost, on this data. It runs before ASR on every input when asr.denoise: true . 1. STFT of the mono signal (window = min(1024, length), scipy.signal.stft) 2. magnitude |S| and phase of every bin 3. noise floor N[f] = 10th percentile of |S[f, t]| over time, per frequency bin 4. cleaned = max(|S| - oversubtraction * N, floor * |S|) 5. keep the original phase; inverse STFT; trim or pad to the input length 6. if peak > 1, scale down to avoid clipping Parameter Default Effect noise_percentile 10.0 Which low percentile of each bin's magnit"},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"The ablation","id":"ablation","x":"Both runs read the identical corpus_noisy/ audio and differ only in asr.denoise ( config.yaml vs. config_denoised.yaml ). Metric Noisy, baseline Noisy, denoised Change 95% CI Corpus WER 0.3377 0.3391 +0.0014 -0.0144 to +0.0174 Corpus CER 0.0964 0.0992 +0.0028 -0.0041 to +0.0099 Mean ASR time (s) 2.054 2.026 -0.028 Mean response F1 (ASR input) 0.1548 0.1547 -0.0001 Correct answers (of 129) 0 0 0 Result: no improvement. Both error rates moved by a hair in the wrong direction, well inside the noise. Latency overhead was negligible (denoising is cheap next to ASR and LLM inference)."},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"Was the default just badly tuned?","id":"sweep","x":"Before accepting a flat result, a quick ASR-only sweep (same checkpoint, no LLM stage, so it runs fast) compared the default against a more aggressive setting on the first 25 questions (all capitals): Setting Mean WER, 25 questions No denoising (baseline) 0.5433 Mild: oversubtraction=1.5, floor=0.05 (the shipped default) 0.5300 Aggressive: oversubtraction=3.0, floor=0.02 0.5567 On this slice mild subtraction helped slightly (about -2.4% relative) and aggressive subtraction made things worse than no denoising. That fits spectral-subtraction theory: over-subtraction leaves isolated spurious spectral peaks (\"musical noise\") that a neural recognizer can handle worse than the original stationary "},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"Is the filter even working?","id":"signal-level","x":"A direct waveform check on one sample confirms it does what it should at the signal level: Quantity Noisy Denoised RMS energy 0.228 0.177 Mean squared error to an independently synthesized clean rendering of the same text 0.094 0.073 (The comparison clip is a separate TTS rendering of the same words, so this is a rough proxy, not a true SNR.) So the negative result is about whether that cleanup survives through the recognizer into fewer errors, not about the code being broken. A Whisper-style model is trained on varied noisy audio and may already be fairly robust to this kind of stationary noise."},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"Takeaway","id":"takeaway","x":"A measurable bottleneck was found and quantified with an interval. An improvement was implemented, unit-tested and evaluated on identical audio. It did not help, and over-tuning hurt. That is reported as a negative result rather than tuned away. Given Part II's other finding (the LLM, not ASR, limits answers), the larger lever is a bigger LLM, not a better front end."},{"p":"extension","t":"Research extension: denoising","h":"Run it","id":"run","x":"python evaluate.py --dataset data/test_noisy.json --config config.yaml --output-dir outputs/evaluation/noisy_baseline --warmup 1 python evaluate.py --dataset data/test_noisy.json --config config_denoised.yaml --output-dir outputs/evaluation/noisy_denoised --warmup 1 python bootstrap.py --a outputs/evaluation/noisy_baseline/report.json --b outputs/evaluation/noisy_denoised/report.json --metric wer"},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"Repository map","id":"","x":"Reference Repository map Every file and folder, with its purpose and whether it is in version control."},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"Layout","id":"tree","x":". |-- asr.py ASR wrapper (Whisper-family), optional denoising |-- llm.py LLM wrapper (chat template, greedy generation) |-- pipeline.py SpeechPipeline + JSON CLI + load_config |-- denoise.py spectral-subtraction front end |-- evaluate.py WER/CER/RTF/F1 metrics + batch evaluation CLI |-- prepare_data.py folder/manifest -> evaluator JSON |-- synthesize_corpus.py TTS corpus generator (clean + noisy) |-- plot_results.py headless plots from evaluation outputs |-- hallucination.py correct / abstained / hallucinated classifier |-- bootstrap.py paired bootstrap intervals |-- verify_task2.py setup checks + mock end-to-end run |-- config.yaml baseline configuration |-- config_denoised.yaml same, with "},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"Source files","id":"source","x":"Path Description Docs asr.py ASRWrapper , resolve_runtime , synchronize Reference llm.py LLMWrapper Reference pipeline.py SpeechPipeline , PipelineResult , load_config , CLI Reference denoise.py spectral_subtract_denoise Extension evaluate.py Metric functions, paired evaluation, report writing, CLI Metrics prepare_data.py Manifest builder Dataset formats synthesize_corpus.py Corpus generator Dataset plot_results.py Plots Plots hallucination.py Fact-based classifier Definition bootstrap.py Bootstrap CIs Method verify_task2.py Verifier Reference"},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"Configuration and project files","id":"config-files","x":"Path Description config.yaml Baseline models, decoding and paths. Reference . config_denoised.yaml Identical but asr.denoise: true . requirements.txt Dependency ranges, checked by verify_task2.py . README.md The repository's operating manual: running, data preparation, evaluation, plotting, verification, hallucination, bootstrap, extension. AGENTS.md Workflow rules: commit each completed logical step with concise Conventional Commits messages; run appropriate checks first; keep generated outputs, model caches, temporary dependencies and local reference documents out of version control. DraftTask1.md The original, longer Task 1 draft (with figure specifications and a verification checklist). "},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"tests/","id":"tests-dir","x":"test_evaluate.py , test_pipeline.py , test_prepare_data.py , test_plot_results.py , test_verify_task2.py , test_denoise.py , test_hallucination.py , test_bootstrap.py . See Tests ."},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"reports/","id":"reports-dir","x":"File Description report.tex , report.pdf The single ACL-style paper (11 pages) covering both parts. task1.md , .tex , .pdf Standalone Task 1 write-up (13 pages), with the three TikZ diagrams. task2.md , .tex , .pdf Standalone Task 2 write-up (7 pages), with the plots and the model-compatibility story."},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"docs/","id":"docs-dir","x":"Path Description *.html The pages of this site, content only. assets/style.css Theme (light and dark), layout, components. assets/app.js Builds the navigation, on-page contents, search, theme toggle and copy buttons. No libraries. assets/search-index.js Generated search index ( python docs/build_search_index.py ). assets/fonts/ Inter variable font (Latin subset, SIL Open Font License 1.1), self-hosted so the site needs no network. assets/img/ Three diagrams cropped from the paper and six result plots (copies of the ignored outputs/plots ). assets/pdf/ Copies of the three PDFs, so the site links work when docs/ is served on its own. build_search_index.py Regenerates the search index from the "},{"p":"files","t":"Repository map","h":"Generated, git-ignored folders","id":"generated","x":"Path Made by Contents corpus_clean/ , corpus_noisy/ synthesize_corpus.py 129 x ( .wav , .txt , .response.txt ) each data/ prepare_data.py test_clean.json , test_noisy.json and metadata files outputs/evaluation/{clean,noisy_baseline,noisy_denoised}/ evaluate.py report.json , samples.csv , failures.csv , wer_vs_quality.csv outputs/evaluation/hallucination.json hallucination.py Correct / abstained / hallucinated counts per condition and arm outputs/plots/<condition>/ plot_results.py wer_vs_quality.png , latency_breakdown.png outputs/verification/ verify_task2.py verification.log , checks.json , summary.md , mock/ .cache/huggingface/ Hugging Face downloads Model weights (about 1 to 3 GB)"},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Glossary and FAQ","id":"","x":"Reference Glossary and FAQ Terms used across the docs, and fixes for the problems most likely to be hit."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Glossary","id":"glossary","x":""},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Evaluation terms","id":"g-eval","x":"Term Meaning WER Word error rate: word-level edits (insertions + deletions + substitutions) divided by reference words. A ratio, can exceed 1. CER Character error rate: the same at the level of Unicode code points (whitespace removed). RTF Real-time factor: processing seconds divided by audio seconds. Below 1 is faster than real time. SNR Signal-to-noise ratio in dB. 0 dB means noise power equals signal power; 8 dB is cleaner. Corpus rate A rate computed from summed edits over summed reference length, so long utterances weigh more than in a per-sample mean. Word-overlap F1 Lexical similarity between a reference and generated answer using multiset word overlap. A proxy, not semantic correctne"},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Speech-model terms","id":"g-models","x":"Term Meaning Speech LLM A language model that takes speech (and often produces speech) directly. Full-duplex Listening and speaking at the same time, with both streams modeled jointly. Moshi / Mimi / Helium The full-duplex model, its causal neural audio codec, and its text LLM backbone. Qwen2.5-Omni: Thinker / Talker The multimodal text LLM, and the speech-generating decoder fed by it. Semantic vs. acoustic tokens Tokens carrying linguistic content vs. those carrying voice and acoustic detail. VQ / RVQ Vector quantization / residual vector quantization: discretizing audio features into codebook indices. DiT, flow matching A diffusion Transformer, and a generative method that transports noise"},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Conversation terms (Part I)","id":"g-conv","x":"Term Meaning Floor Who currently \"has the turn\" in a conversation. Backchannel A listener's short acknowledgment (\"mm-hm\") that does not take the floor. Hesitation pause A pause inside a speaker's own turn; the speaker is not finished. Genuine interruption The user takes the floor while the assistant is speaking. Continue / hold / yield The controller's three actions: keep talking, briefly pause output, or give up the floor and clear pending audio. False yield Giving up the floor when the assistant should have kept it. Missed interruption Failing to stop within the deadline after a genuine interruption. Interruption-to-yield latency Time from annotated interruption onset to actual playback s"},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"FAQ and troubleshooting","id":"faq","x":""},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"UnicodeEncodeError: 'charmap' codec can't encode characters","id":"q-unicode","x":"Windows consoles often use cp1252, which cannot print Telugu. Write results to a file, or run set PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 (PowerShell: $env:PYTHONIOENCODING = \"utf-8\" ) before running. The code itself writes UTF-8 JSON and CSV."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"ValueError: The generation config is outdated...","id":"q-generation-config","x":"You passed language or task to a checkpoint whose generation_config.json predates that API. Set asr.language: null for single-language fine-tunes. See Pipeline ."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"My ASR output is in the wrong script and WER is above 1","id":"q-script","x":"You are probably using a general multilingual checkpoint (such as openai/whisper-small ) for Telugu. Use a Telugu-specific model, or a larger general one ( whisper-large-v3 ) if memory allows. A WER above 1 is a signal to look at the raw transcripts."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"CUDA out of memory","id":"q-oom","x":"Both models stay loaded at once. Use smaller checkpoints (the defaults already are), set runtime.device: cpu (slow), or free GPU memory. Whisper-large-v3 plus Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct needs roughly 18 GB in fp16."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'soundfile'","id":"q-modules","x":"Dependencies are not installed in the active Python. Run python -m pip install -r requirements.txt and confirm python is the interpreter you installed into."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"No module named 'tests.test_...'","id":"q-tests","x":"The tests folder is not a package. Use python -m unittest discover -s tests -v , or add -p test_name.py for one file."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Warnings I can ignore","id":"q-warnings","x":"\"unauthenticated requests to the HF Hub\" : set HF_TOKEN for higher rate limits; downloads still work without it. \"Using chunk_length_s is very experimental with seq2seq models\" : transformers' general caution about chunked Whisper decoding. The clips here are short, well under 30 s. \"torch_dtype is deprecated\" and \"Both max_new_tokens and max_length seem to have been set\" : library deprecation notices; max_new_tokens takes precedence. \"generation flags are not valid and may be ignored: temperature, top_p\" : sampling flags unused under greedy decoding."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"git push fails with HTTP 408 or \"Connection was reset\"","id":"q-push","x":"That is the network path to GitHub, not a repository problem (reads still work). Try again on a steadier connection, and turn off any VPN or tunnel (such as Cloudflare WARP) that may be interfering with large uploads. Local commits are safe until the push succeeds."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Why are my numbers slightly different from the docs?","id":"q-numbers","x":"Fresh TTS synthesis is not seeded, so the audio differs. See what is deterministic ."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Which PDF is the main report?","id":"q-onepdf","x":"reports/report.pdf , the single paper. task1.pdf and task2.pdf are longer companions."},{"p":"glossary","t":"Glossary and FAQ","h":"Does Part II test Part I's proposal?","id":"q-proposal","x":"No. Part II measures a cascaded pipeline, which has no floor-control decision. The proposal is specified but not run; see the proposal page ."},{"p":"index","t":"Speech AI Report","h":"Speech AI Report","id":"","x":"Speech AI Report An architectural analysis of two speech LLMs, a proposal for conversational floor control, and a reproducible Telugu speech-to-text-response pipeline with its own evaluation code, results and limitations. Author: Banuru Mohammed Fawaz. These pages document everything in the repository: the analysis, the code, the data, the metrics, the results and the decisions behind them."},{"p":"index","t":"Speech AI Report","h":"Headline results","id":"headline","x":"All Part II numbers are measured on 129 synthesized Telugu utterances per condition, with 0 failed samples in every run. Part I contains no experiments. 0.293 Corpus WER, clean CER 0.075 0.338 Corpus WER, noisy (0 dB SNR) +0.045, 95% CI +0.014 to +0.077 +0.001 WER change from denoising 95% CI -0.014 to +0.017, no effect 0 / 129 Correct answers from speech 3 / 129 from perfect transcripts 0 Abstentions in 774 answers The LLM never says \"I don't know\" 4.15 Pipeline real-time factor, clean ASR alone: 0.50. The LLM is about 88% of time. The one-line takeaway The ASR stage is fine and real-time. The small 1.5B LLM is the dominant failure, and it fails confidently rather than abstaining. Noise mea"},{"p":"index","t":"Speech AI Report","h":"The two parts","id":"parts","x":"Part I: Architecture analysis Moshi vs. Qwen2.5-Omni along seven axes, three ranked limitations, and why \"end of speech\" is really four decisions. Part I: Yielding proposal A falsifiable experiment: a small controller on frozen Moshi that uses generation state to continue, hold or yield. Specified, not run. Part II: Pipeline Telugu speech to ASR to LLM to text response, the full config reference, and how timing is measured. Part II: Results Clean vs. noisy tables, latency distributions, hallucination rate, confidence intervals and all six plots. Keep the two parts separate Part II measures a cascade , which has no floor-control decision to evaluate. It does not test Part I's hypothesis, and "},{"p":"index","t":"Speech AI Report","h":"Deliverables","id":"deliverables","x":"File What it is report.pdf The single ACL-style paper combining both parts (11 pages). task1.pdf Longer standalone Task 1 write-up (13 pages) with the three diagrams. task2.pdf Longer standalone Task 2 write-up (7 pages) with all plots. GitHub repository All code, configs, tests and reports."},{"p":"index","t":"Speech AI Report","h":"Where to find things","id":"map","x":"I want to... Go to Run it Quickstart See how each requirement is met Requirements Understand a config key Config reference Know how a metric is defined Metrics and evaluation Look up a function or CLI flag Modules and CLI Learn why a decision was made Decision log See what could be wrong or missing Limitations Find a file Repository map Press / or Ctrl + K anywhere to search every page."},{"p":"limitations","t":"Limitations","h":"Limitations","id":"","x":"Reference Limitations What these results and analyses do not show. Each item says how much it matters and what would fix it."},{"p":"limitations","t":"Limitations","h":"Part I: analysis and proposal","id":"part1","x":"Limitation Weight What would address it No experiment was run. The yielding-controller hypothesis is specified but untested; no gain, dataset size, parameter count or runtime is claimed. Fundamental Implement the controller, collect and annotate the data, run the constrained comparison on the proposal page . Statements about the models rest on two reports. Later releases or code may differ, and reports omit many quantities (see what is unspecified ). Moderate Check against released code and checkpoints. The three limitations are ranked by judgment , not measured. Moderate Component-level timing and event-level evaluation of the real systems. Implementation access is unverified: whether Moshi"},{"p":"limitations","t":"Limitations","h":"Part II: the Telugu pipeline","id":"part2","x":"Limitation Weight What would address it Synthetic speech only. All audio is TTS output. Nothing here speaks to accents, prosody, disfluency, speaking rate or microphone channels. Fundamental Evaluate on real recorded Telugu (an exported FLEURS or IndicVoices subset can be fed through prepare_data.py as a manifest). Small models, by memory necessity. A larger ASR/LLM pair would likely change both WER and, above all, factual accuracy. The 0/129 result is specific to Qwen2.5-1.5B. Fundamental A GPU with room for Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (or quantization) and Whisper-large-v3. Templated questions. Only capitals and additions, short utterances, one fact each. Moderate Open-ended or multi-turn question"},{"p":"limitations","t":"Limitations","h":"What can and cannot be claimed","id":"claims","x":"Supported The pipeline runs end to end. WER is 0.293 clean. Noise raises WER by a real, interval-backed amount. Denoising does not measurably change it. From speech, the 1.5B LLM never gave the right fact. Suggestive Aggressive spectral subtraction is worse than none (25-question slice only). The LLM, not ASR, is the main lever for quality. Not shown That the yielding controller works. That results hold on real speech, other languages, bigger models, or other noise. That cascades are slower than end-to-end systems."},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Metrics and evaluation","id":"","x":"Part II: Pipeline Metrics and evaluation The evaluation infrastructure is written from scratch in this repository. No evaluation library is used: edit distance, error rates, latency accounting, response scoring, hallucination classification and bootstrap intervals are all written in this repository."},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Text normalization","id":"normalization","x":"Every reference and hypothesis passes through the same function before scoring: Unicode NFC normalization, then whitespace collapsed to single spaces. Case and punctuation are kept (Telugu has no case; punctuation differences count). NFC matters for Telugu because vowel signs and combining marks can be encoded in more than one equivalent way."},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"WER and CER","id":"wer-cer","x":"WER = word_edits / reference_words CER = character_edits / reference_characters word units = normalized text split on whitespace character units = Unicode code points of the text with whitespace removed edits = unit-cost insertions + deletions + substitutions (Levenshtein) The edit distance is a two-row dynamic program, O(N*M) time and O(min(N, M)) memory, implemented from scratch. Both are ratios, not percentages , and can exceed 1 because of insertions (the discarded whisper-small run reached a corpus WER of 2.56). CER counts code points, not grapheme clusters, so a Telugu consonant plus vowel sign counts as two units. This is applied identically to reference and hypothesis. An empty refer"},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Corpus vs. per-sample","id":"corpus-vs-sample","x":"Corpus WER/CER (the headline) = total edits over all samples divided by total reference length, so longer questions weigh more. The report also gives per-sample mean, median (p50) and 95th percentile, using linear interpolation between ranks."},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Latency and real-time factor","id":"latency","x":"Metric Definition asr_seconds , llm_seconds , total_seconds Wall clock with CUDA synchronization. Includes decoding, resampling, optional denoising and generation. Excludes model loading and metadata reads. rtf total_seconds / audio_seconds for one sample. Below 1 means faster than real time. asr_rtf asr_seconds / audio_seconds . ASR stage alone. corpus_rtf Total pipeline seconds divided by total audio seconds over successful samples. Distributions Mean, p50, p95 of each stage and of end-to-end time. Audio duration is read from file metadata (frames / sample rate) without decoding samples. The extra \"clean transcript\" LLM call used for the paired comparison is excluded from pipeline latency "},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Response quality","id":"quality","x":"The default score is multiset word-overlap F1 in [0, 1], a transparent lexical proxy: overlap = sum over words of min(count in reference, count in hypothesis) F1 = 2 * overlap / (|reference words| + |hypothesis words|) both empty -> 1; exactly one empty -> 0; word order is ignored It is not semantic correctness: a valid paraphrase or a different inflection can score low, and a wrong answer that shares words with the reference can score higher than it deserves. That is why the stricter fact test exists. A custom scorer (higher is better, finite, in [0, 1]) can be passed programmatically and is then applied to both arms. Exact-match rates (normalized equality with the reference answer) are als"},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"The paired error-propagation comparison","id":"paired","x":"Each question is answered twice by the same LLM, with deterministic decoding and no chat history, and both answers are scored against the same human reference answer: Audio → ASR → LLM → answer from ASR transcript Human transcript → same LLM → answer from perfect transcript quality_drop = clean_response_quality - asr_response_quality . Positive means ASR errors hurt; negative means the ASR arm scored better. degraded_fraction and improved_fraction : share of samples where the drop is above or below zero (tolerance 1e-12). response_agreement_f1 : F1 between the two arms' answers. The clean-arm answer is a baseline, not the scoring truth. The truth is always the human-written reference. This i"},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Correctness and hallucination rate","id":"hallucination","x":"The requirements ask for \"response correctness and hallucination rate using clearly defined evaluation criteria\". Because each reference answer states exactly one fact, a response can be classified against it with a rule that needs no human judge. hallucination.py does this post hoc from saved report.json files, with no model inference. Item Definition Key fact For a question_cap-* audio file: the capital name (last word of the reference answer). For question_sum-* : the sum word (the word before వస్తుంది ). correct The normalized response contains the key fact as a substring. For sums, the digit form also counts, matched as a whole number (so \"5\" does not match inside \"15\"). abstained Not c"},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Confidence intervals","id":"bootstrap","x":"bootstrap.py gives a 95% paired bootstrap interval for the difference in corpus WER or CER between two systems: Pair the two reports' rows by sample ID (same question in both). Resample the question indices with replacement, 10,000 times, with a fixed seed. For each resample, recompute each system's corpus rate (sum of edits / sum of reference units) and take the difference. Report the point difference and the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. An interval that contains 0 means the difference is not distinguishable from question-sampling noise. It does not capture speaker or recording variation, since the question set has none."},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Failure handling","id":"failures","x":"A sample whose pipeline raises (for example an empty ASR transcript) is recorded in failures.csv and excluded from every aggregate . samples_failed and failure_rate sit next to the metrics, because a successful-only average can look better than the truth. The CLI still saves partial reports and exits with status 1; --fail-fast stops at the first failure instead. In the reported runs, 0 of 129 samples failed in every condition."},{"p":"metrics","t":"Metrics and evaluation","h":"Evaluator outputs","id":"outputs","x":"File Contents report.json Metadata, configuration, summary metrics, every paired output, per-sample measurements and failures. Strict UTF-8 JSON. samples.csv One row per sample with all measurements (UTF-8 with BOM for spreadsheets). failures.csv Sample id, path, error type and message. wer_vs_quality.csv Plotting points: WER, ASR and clean response quality, quality drop."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"Modules and CLI","id":"","x":"Reference Modules and CLI Every Python file in the repository, what it exposes, and how to call it. All scripts are run from the repository root. About 1,990 lines of code in 11 modules, plus 8 test files. Module Lines Purpose asr.py 144 Whisper ASR wrapper with timing llm.py 96 Chat-LLM wrapper with timing pipeline.py 127 Composes ASR and LLM; JSON CLI; config loader denoise.py 68 Spectral-subtraction front end evaluate.py 394 Metrics and the batch evaluation CLI prepare_data.py 201 Builds evaluator manifests from folders or tables synthesize_corpus.py 140 TTS corpus generator (clean and noisy) plot_results.py 202 Headless plots from evaluation outputs hallucination.py 121 Correct / abstain"},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"asr.py","id":"asr","x":"Whisper-compatible ASR with local audio decoding and synchronized timing. Inputs supported by libsndfile (WAV, FLAC) work without FFmpeg. Functions resolve_runtime(device, dtype) -> (torch.device, torch.dtype) : resolves auto , validates the accelerator and precision. Shared with llm.py . synchronize(device) : waits for queued CUDA/MPS work before a clock read. class ASRWrapper ASRWrapper(model_name=\"openai/whisper-large-v3\", *, device=\"auto\", dtype=\"auto\", cache_dir=None, revision=\"main\", language=\"telugu\", chunk_length_s=30, batch_size=1, generation=None, denoise=False) Loads the processor and model, rejects non-Whisper architectures, builds a Hugging Face automatic-speech-recognition pipe"},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"llm.py","id":"llm","x":"class LLMWrapper LLMWrapper(model_name=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\", *, device=\"auto\", dtype=\"auto\", cache_dir=None, revision=\"main\", system_prompt=\"Answer clearly and concisely in Telugu script.\", max_input_tokens=2048, generation=None) Requires a tokenizer with a chat template (system and user roles); otherwise raises. generate(prompt) -> (response, elapsed_seconds) : builds the chat messages, tokenizes, rejects prompts over max_input_tokens or overflowing the model context, generates, and decodes only the newly generated tokens . No history is retained. Sets pad_token_id from the pad or EOS token if missing. Drops temperature and top_p when do_sample is false."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"pipeline.py","id":"pipeline-py","x":"PipelineResult (frozen dataclass): audio_path, transcript, response, asr_seconds, llm_seconds, total_seconds . SpeechPipeline(asr, llm) : accepts any objects with the same methods (used for testing). from_config(config) loads both models. run(audio_path) runs both stages and raises on an empty transcript. load_config(path) : safe YAML load, validates that sections are mappings, resolves paths.audio / output / cache_dir relative to the YAML's directory. python pipeline.py [--config config.yaml] [--audio FILE] [--output FILE] --audio and --output override the config, resolved from the current directory. Prints the JSON and saves it. Exits with status 1 on failure."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"denoise.py","id":"denoise","x":"spectral_subtract_denoise(audio, sample_rate, *, noise_percentile=10.0, oversubtraction=1.5, floor=0.05) -> np.ndarray Returns a denoised float32 copy of a 1-D mono waveform. Validates input and parameters. See the extension page for the algorithm."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"evaluate.py","id":"evaluate","x":"Metric functions (standard library only) Name What it does normalize_text(text) NFC and collapsed whitespace. levenshtein_distance(ref, hyp) Unit-cost edit distance over any token sequences, two-row DP. ErrorRate(edits, reference_units) Frozen dataclass; .rate is None for an empty reference. transcript_errors(ref, hyp) {\"wer\": ErrorRate, \"cer\": ErrorRate} word_error_rate , character_error_rate Convenience wrappers returning the float or None . response_token_f1(ref, hyp) Multiset word-overlap F1. real_time_factor(total_s, audio_s) Validated ratio. audio_duration(path) Duration from file metadata via soundfile. Dataset and evaluation EvaluationSample(id, audio_path, ground_truth_transcript, g"},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"prepare_data.py","id":"prepare","x":"Builds the evaluator JSON from a local audio folder (with .txt and .response.txt sidecars) or a CSV, TSV or JSON manifest. Works only with local files; it does not download datasets or detect the language. python prepare_data.py --source SRC [--output data/test.json] [--limit 100] [--seed 42] [--response-mode require|placeholder] [--audio-column audio_path] [--transcript-column ground_truth_transcript] [--response-column ground_truth_response] [--overwrite] --limit 0 selects every sample; otherwise a seeded random subset (default 100). --response-mode placeholder fills only missing answers with a fixed Telugu test string and flags the dataset as invalid for response-quality scoring. Refuses "},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"synthesize_corpus.py","id":"synth","x":"python synthesize_corpus.py [--clean-out corpus_clean] [--noisy-out corpus_noisy] [--snr-db 8.0] [--limit 0] [--skip-clean] Constants: TTS_MODEL_NAME = \"facebook/mms-tts-tel\" , TARGET_SNR_DB = 8.0 (the default; the reported runs use --snr-db 0 ), NOISE_SEED = 42 . build_qa_pairs() returns the 129 (id, question, answer) triples. add_noise(signal, snr_db, rng) adds Gaussian noise at a fixed SNR. --limit N synthesizes only the first N pairs. --skip-clean still synthesizes each clean waveform in memory but writes only the noisy copy."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"plot_results.py","id":"plot","x":"python plot_results.py --input report.json|samples.csv|wer_vs_quality.csv [--output-dir outputs/plots] [--quality-label \"Word overlap F1\"] Matplotlib only, rendered headless. Produces wer_vs_quality.png (both series against ASR WER) and latency_breakdown.png (stacked mean ASR, LLM and overhead, plus the clean-transcript LLM time as a separate bar). JSON input also reports the failed-sample count; CSV input cannot. Undefined points are omitted with counts in the legend; malformed numbers and incomplete latency records are rejected. It accepts only evaluation outputs, not the prepared dataset JSON. Existing plot files in the output directory are replaced."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"hallucination.py","id":"halluc","x":"python hallucination.py --reports R1.json [R2.json ...] [--output OUT.json] extract_key_fact(audio_path, reference_answer) : capital name or sum word, chosen by the audio file prefix. accepted_forms(audio_path, key_fact) : the word, plus the digit form for sums. classify_response(response, key_fact_or_forms) : correct / abstained / hallucinated . fact_rates(samples, response_field) and analyze_report(path) : counts and rates for the ASR-transcript arm and the human-transcript arm. The condition name in the output is the report's parent folder name. Constant: UNCERTAINTY_MARKERS (three Telugu phrases)."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"bootstrap.py","id":"boot","x":"python bootstrap.py --a REF.json --b COMPARE.json [--metric wer|cer] [--resamples 10000] [--seed 0] paired_bootstrap(edits_a, units_a, edits_b, units_b, *, resamples, seed, level) returns difference (B minus A), ci_low , ci_high , samples . compare_reports(path_a, path_b, metric) pairs rows by sample ID and errors if the two reports do not cover the same IDs."},{"p":"modules","t":"Modules and CLI","h":"verify_task2.py","id":"verify","x":"python verify_task2.py [--config config.yaml] [--output-dir outputs/verification] Checks: required source files exist and parse; installed dependencies match requirements.txt ranges; configuration types and ranges (including Telugu ASR settings and deterministic decoding); device availability; write access to the cache, output and default data , outputs/evaluation , outputs/plots folders. Then it generates exactly three silent dummy WAVs and runs the real SpeechPipeline and evaluate_dataset with injected stand-ins whose outputs and clocks are synthetic, comparing results with fixed expected values for perfect, substitution and deletion cases plus insertion, empty-reference and Unicode-normal"},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"Pipeline and configuration","id":"","x":"Part II: Pipeline Pipeline and configuration A cascaded system: Telugu speech in, a Telugu text answer out, with a Whisper-family recognizer feeding an instruction-tuned LLM. Both models stay loaded and are reused across a whole evaluation."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"Architecture","id":"architecture","x":"Speech input WAV / FLAC → Denoise optional, asr.denoise → ASR vasista22/whisper-telugu-small → LLM Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct → Telugu text response File Role config.yaml Models, revisions, device, precision, decoding, paths. asr.py ASRWrapper : decodes audio with soundfile, averages to mono, optionally denoises, resamples to the model's rate, transcribes with a Hugging Face ASR pipeline. Timing is synchronized. llm.py LLMWrapper : applies the chat template (system + user), generates, returns only the newly generated text. Stateless: no history is kept between calls. pipeline.py SpeechPipeline composes the two, rejects an empty transcript before calling the LLM, and exposes the JSON CLI. denoise."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"What is timed","id":"timing","x":"asr_seconds : audio decoding, mono mix, optional denoising, resampling and transcription. Excludes model loading. llm_seconds : prompt tokenization, generation and decoding. Excludes model loading. total_seconds : wall clock around both stages, so it can exceed their sum slightly (pipeline overhead). CUDA work is synchronized before each clock read. The first inference may include kernel initialization; the evaluator therefore runs --warmup 1 by default in the documented commands."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"Model choices","id":"models","x":"Stage Used Original default Why changed ASR vasista22/whisper-telugu-small openai/whisper-large-v3 Large-v3 plus a 7B LLM do not fit in about 6.5 GB free VRAM. The general openai/whisper-small was tried and rejected: see below. LLM Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct Memory (about 3 GB fp16 vs. about 15 GB). Why not the general whisper-small Even with Telugu forced, openai/whisper-small transcribed this corpus's Telugu audio into Devanagari-looking text and looped on short clips. The first full clean run under it scored corpus WER 2.56 and CER 2.69, and was discarded. The Telugu-only fine-tune fixed it (WER 0.293). Full story in the decision log . Why language: null The Telug"},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"Config reference: config.yaml","id":"config","x":"Relative filesystem paths resolve from the YAML file's directory. CLI path overrides resolve from the current directory."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"paths","id":"cfg-paths","x":"Key Default Meaning paths.audio data/sample_te.wav Default input for pipeline.py when --audio is not given. No file is bundled. paths.output outputs/result.json Where the pipeline's JSON result is saved (UTF-8, Telugu preserved). paths.cache_dir .cache/huggingface Hugging Face model cache."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"runtime","id":"cfg-runtime","x":"Key Default Meaning runtime.device auto auto , cpu , cuda , cuda:N or mps . auto prefers CUDA, then MPS, then CPU. Errors if a requested accelerator is unavailable. runtime.dtype auto float32 , float16 or bfloat16 . auto is float16 on CUDA, float32 elsewhere. float16 on CPU is rejected."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"asr","id":"cfg-asr","x":"Key Value used Meaning asr.model_name vasista22/whisper-telugu-small Any Transformers-compatible Whisper (or IndicWhisper) checkpoint or local directory. Non-Whisper architectures are rejected. asr.revision main Model commit. Left unpinned for this checkpoint; see Limitations . asr.language null See the warning above. A value like telugu forces the language and sets task . asr.chunk_length_s 30 Chunking window for the ASR pipeline. Must be positive. asr.batch_size 1 Must be at least 1. The wrapper handles one request at a time. asr.denoise false Spectral-subtraction front end. true only in config_denoised.yaml . asr.generation.max_new_tokens 256 Decoder cap. (Transformers also warns that max"},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"llm","id":"cfg-llm","x":"Key Value used Meaning llm.model_name Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct A causal instruction model with a chat template supporting system and user roles. Base models need their own prompt adapter. llm.revision 989aa7980e4cf806f80c7fef2b1adb7bc71aa306 Pinned commit. llm.system_prompt \"You are a helpful assistant. Answer the user's question clearly and concisely in Telugu using Telugu script. If the request is unclear, ask for clarification. Do not invent facts.\" The prompt the hallucination test measures against: it explicitly allows abstaining. llm.max_input_tokens 2048 Longer prompts raise an error rather than being silently truncated. llm.generation.max_new_tokens 256 Response budget. Prompt plus"},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"The two config files","id":"two-configs","x":"config_denoised.yaml is identical to config.yaml except for asr.denoise: true (and slightly shorter comments, with asr.revision: main noted as unpinned). It exists so the baseline-vs-improved ablation differs in exactly one setting."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"Output schema","id":"output","x":"pipeline.py prints and saves one JSON object ( PipelineResult ): Field Type Meaning audio_path string Resolved path of the input file. transcript string ASR output. response string LLM answer to the transcript. asr_seconds float See what is timed . llm_seconds float total_seconds float These records are evaluation inputs, not scores. WER and response quality need reference labels, which is what the evaluator adds."},{"p":"pipeline","t":"Pipeline and configuration","h":"Built-in guards","id":"guards","x":"An empty ASR transcript raises an error and the LLM is not called. Audio must be finite and non-empty. Stereo is averaged to mono. Audio is resampled to the model's actual rate ( scipy.signal.resample_poly ), not just relabeled. Over-long prompts and prompt-plus-budget overflow raise errors rather than truncating Telugu text or dropping chat-control tokens. Each pipeline instance is intended for sequential use."},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","id":"","x":"Part I: Analysis Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding The proposed improvement: a small causal controller on frozen Moshi that reads the model's own generation state to decide continue, hold or yield, specified as an experiment that could fail. Status: specified, not run No result is claimed. There is no controller implementation, no collected data and no measurement behind this page. It is a research design, and its value is in being fully specified and refutable."},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"The failure it targets","id":"problem","x":"A speech-activity rule (\"stop when the user starts making sound\") can treat a listener's \"mm-hm\" as an attempt to take the floor. That is a hypothesized baseline failure, not an established failure of either paper's model. The proposal asks whether the speech LLM's own internal state tells the two cases apart better than audio and playback cues alone."},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Hypothesis and refutation","id":"hypothesis","x":"Hypothesis A frozen speech LLM's own generation state improves continue/hold/yield decisions beyond incoming audio and observable playback history. It predicts fewer false yields on eligible backchannel and hesitation events than speech-activity rules and an audio-only learned controller, under matched interruption-to-yield latency and missed-interruption constraints. Refuted if the state-aware controller cannot improve that constrained comparison on held-out speakers and conversations, or if removing generation state preserves the benefit. Four secondary alternatives remain open: whether buffering dominates onset latency, whether codec phonetic quality predicts interaction accuracy, whether"},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"The controller","id":"controller","x":"Attach a small causal controller to frozen Moshi. The base model's parameters never change."},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Inputs (decision-time only)","id":"inputs","x":"Current Mimi representations and causal speech-activity features. Observable assistant playback activity, elapsed activity and silence durations, queue occupancy. Generation-state additions: Moshi's Temporal Transformer context vector and the self-text PAD probability (M §3.4.1, pp.13-14; §3.4.4, pp.16-17). Only values available at decision time may enter. Future audio or future generated text must never be a feature (it may inform labels only)."},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Actions","id":"actions","x":"Action Effect continue Release available audio. hold Briefly suspend release while listening continues. Duration is capped on development data; excessive holding counts as disruption, not as a successful non-yield. yield Suppress output and clear pending playback while listening continues. Playback resumes only through a later controller decision. All actions go through one bounded playback actuator. A learned projection plus a recurrent classifier produces the action. The actuator controls the queue-to-speaker path only; it must never interrupt the incoming-audio path. The contribution under test is the use of generation state , not turn-taking prediction itself."},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Training and labeled data","id":"data","x":"Train only the projection and classifier, with supervised action cross-entropy. Labels: event type, floor ownership, event onset, acceptable action intervals, interruption-response deadlines. Annotators hear the surrounding dialogue and mark ambiguity independently of controller output. Data: consented human-Moshi conversations plus controlled reenactments, recorded as separate user and actually-played-assistant channels, then replayed through frozen Moshi for causal feature traces. Fisher's separate channels are a possible auxiliary source for human conversational cues (M §4.2, p.18); new action annotations and human-system validation are still required. Split by speaker, conversation and s"},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Experimental design","id":"design","x":"Condition Audio cues Playback history Moshi generation state Thresholds Unmodified Moshi Native Native Native No added controller Fixed speech-activity rule Yes Same actuator No Prespecified Tuned speech-activity rule Yes Same actuator No Development set Audio-only learned controller Yes Yes No Development set State-aware learned controller Yes Yes Yes Development set Winning only against the fixed rule would not show a generation-state benefit; the tuned rule and the audio-only learned controller exist to rule out calibration and \"any learned classifier helps\" explanations. Figure 3. Experimental design matrix. No empirical curve is implied; all rows are proposed conditions, not measured re"},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Primary metric and constraints","id":"metric","x":"Events are matched backchannel, hesitation-pause and genuine-interruption scenarios, balanced across speakers and recording conditions. Floor intent is inferred from context, not from event duration alone. Completed questions are an endpoint sanity check outside the primary comparison. false-yield rate = (events with an inappropriate yield or excessive hold) / (eligible floor-retention events) primary metric = unweighted mean of the backchannel and hesitation false-yield rates (both also reported separately, with denominators) missed-interruption rate = (genuine interruptions without timely playback cessation) / (genuine interruptions) Interruption-to-yield latency: from annotated interrupti"},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Ablation, robustness and controls","id":"ablation","x":"Diagnostic ablation: remove generation-state features but keep acoustic features, playback history, queue state, classifier capacity and training budget. If the benefit survives, the proposed contribution is rejected even if the controller still beats the rule. Report any parameter-count difference from the input projection. Robustness: repeat held-out events under identical noise and echo perturbations. Report activity-detection errors and controller errors separately. Indic transfer: a Hindi/Telugu subset with fluent annotators and per-language agreement. Evaluate zero-shot transfer before any adaptation and report the two separately. Do not assume either base model fails on Indic speech, "},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Replay vs. interactive evaluation","id":"replay","x":"For immediate-action comparisons, start every system from a common assistant prefix and replay the same user event, so differences are not blamed on different user recordings. Replay cannot reproduce how people react to different assistant actions, so follow-up live trials with counterbalanced order must assess resumption coherence and dialogue drift. The two kinds of outcome are reported separately and never merged into one causal claim."},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Threats to validity","id":"threats","x":"Threat Mitigation in the design Generation state encodes only timing or speaker identity Playback history and durations supplied to both learned variants; speaker-disjoint splits Threshold tuning explains the gain The development-tuned rule baseline Extra waiting, not better decisions, lowers false yields Common latency limits and explicit hold accounting Scripted cues explain the performance Natural held-out conversations Dialogue drift: Moshi can internally generate speech that suppression stops the user hearing Freezing the model does not repair its internal dialogue state. Track unheard output, resumption coherence, truncation and controller overhead as follow-up outcomes. Failure there "},{"p":"proposal","t":"Proposal: generation-state-aware yielding","h":"Open items to verify before running","id":"verify","x":"Whether an implementation exposes the causal Mimi representations, temporal context and PAD probabilities, and what Moshi's state does when generated audio is suppressed from playback. Fisher access and permitted use before selecting it as an auxiliary annotation source. Controller and feature-extraction overhead, and audible discontinuities after suppression and resumption. \"Lightweight\" is a design target until measured. Novelty: whether prior systems already use a speech generator's hidden state for conversational action selection. TurnGPT (text-based turn-taking prediction) and Voice Activity Projection (audio-based voice-activity prediction) show turn prediction predates this proposal. "},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Quickstart","id":"","x":"Start Quickstart From a clean checkout to a transcript, a test run, and the full reproduction of the reported results."},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Requirements","id":"requirements","x":"Python 3.10 or newer (the reported run used 3.13.4). Internet on first use, to download model weights from Hugging Face (roughly 1 to 3 GB in total). A GPU is optional but strongly recommended. The reported run used an RTX 4060 Laptop (8 GB, about 6.5 GB free). CPU works but is slow, mostly because of the LLM stage. Telugu text in the console: on Windows, printing Telugu to a cp1252 console raises UnicodeEncodeError . Write output to a file or set PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 ."},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Install","id":"install","x":"python -m pip install -r requirements.txt GPU builds of PyTorch requirements.txt lists plain torch . For an NVIDIA GPU, install the CUDA build of PyTorch for your system first, then run the command above. The reported run used torch 2.11.0+cu128 and transformers 4.57.6 (the pin allows >=4.45,<6 )."},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Run the pipeline on one recording","id":"run-one","x":"No sample audio is bundled. Use any real Telugu WAV or FLAC file (other formats depend on your libsndfile). python pipeline.py --audio \"path/to/question.wav\" It prints JSON and saves it to paths.output from the config (default outputs/result.json ). Fields: audio_path , transcript , response , asr_seconds , llm_seconds , total_seconds . See pipeline output . Or from Python: from pipeline import SpeechPipeline, load_config pipeline = SpeechPipeline.from_config(load_config(\"config.yaml\")) result = pipeline.run(\"path/to/question.wav\") print(result.transcript, result.response, result.total_seconds)"},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Run the tests","id":"tests","x":"All 30 tests are offline: no model downloads, no GPU. python -m unittest discover -s tests -v Use -s tests (not python -m unittest tests.test_x ), because the test folder is not a package. Details on the tests page ."},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Check your setup","id":"verify","x":"python verify_task2.py --config config.yaml --output-dir outputs/verification Checks required files, dependency versions, configuration, device and output folders, then runs the real evaluator on three dummy samples with injected stand-ins and fixed expected values. It writes verification.log , checks.json and summary.md . The timings in its mock table are synthetic by design. It does not prove model quality or memory fit."},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Reproduce every reported number","id":"reproduce","x":"Run from the repository root, in order. The synthesized corpora and outputs/ are not committed, so these commands regenerate them. # 1. synthesize the Telugu corpus (clean + 0 dB noisy) python synthesize_corpus.py --clean-out corpus_clean --noisy-out corpus_noisy --snr-db 0 # 2. build evaluator manifests python prepare_data.py --source corpus_clean --output data/test_clean.json --limit 0 --seed 42 python prepare_data.py --source corpus_noisy --output data/test_noisy.json --limit 0 --seed 42 # 3. evaluate three conditions (each loads both models once) python evaluate.py --dataset data/test_clean.json --config config.yaml --output-dir outputs/evaluation/clean --warmup 1 python evaluate.py --da"},{"p":"quickstart","t":"Quickstart","h":"Rebuild the paper","id":"report","x":"cd reports pdflatex report.tex pdflatex report.tex Run it twice for cross-references. The figures read the plots under outputs/plots/ , so regenerate those first. See Reproducibility for the full environment."},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"Reproducibility","id":"","x":"Reference Reproducibility Everything needed to rerun the experiments and know how close a rerun should land."},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"Environment of the reported runs","id":"environment","x":"OS Windows 11 (PowerShell and Git Bash) Python 3.13.4 PyTorch 2.11.0+cu128 transformers 4.57.6 (allowed range >=4.45,<6 ) Other dependencies numpy >=1.26, scipy >=1.11, soundfile >=0.12, PyYAML >=6, matplotlib >=3.8, packaging >=23 (see requirements.txt ) GPU NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060 Laptop, 8 GB total, about 6.5 GB free Precision float16 on CUDA ( dtype: auto ) Decoding Greedy for both ASR ( num_beams: 1 ) and LLM ( do_sample: false ), plus one warm-up pass LaTeX (for the PDFs) MiKTeX 25.12, pdflatex"},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"Models and revisions","id":"models","x":"Role Model Revision ASR vasista22/whisper-telugu-small main (unpinned; see Limitations ) LLM Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct 989aa7980e4cf806f80c7fef2b1adb7bc71aa306 TTS (data generation) facebook/mms-tts-tel main"},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"What is and is not deterministic","id":"determinism","x":"Step Deterministic? Detail Question text and reference answers Yes Fixed lists and arithmetic in synthesize_corpus.py . TTS audio No The TTS model's duration prediction is not seeded, so a fresh synthesis renders different audio for the same text. Noise addition Yes, given the clean audio NumPy generator seeded with 42. Dataset subset selection Yes Seeded (42); with --limit 0 every sample is used. ASR and LLM output Yes, for fixed audio and hardware Greedy decoding. Not guaranteed bit-identical across hardware or library versions. Latency numbers No Wall-clock time varies run to run and across machines. Bootstrap intervals Yes Seeded (0 by default). Expectation for a rerun Because the TTS au"},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"Commands","id":"commands","x":"The full ordered command list is on the Quickstart . In short: synthesize the corpus at --snr-db 0 , build both manifests with --limit 0 , run evaluate.py three times (clean; noisy with config.yaml ; noisy with config_denoised.yaml ), then plots, hallucination.py and bootstrap.py ."},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"What is and is not in the repository","id":"outputs","x":"Committed Git-ignored (regenerate or obtain locally) All source, tests, both configs, README.md , requirements.txt , AGENTS.md , DraftTask1.md , the reports/ sources and PDFs, this docs/ site outputs/ (evaluation reports, plots), data/ (manifests), corpus_clean/ , corpus_noisy/ , .cache/ (model weights), tmp/ , __pycache__/ , .venv/ , and the local copies of the two source papers ( moshi.pdf , qwen omni.pdf ) The result plots and diagrams on this site are copies stored in docs/assets/img/ so the site works without the ignored folders."},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"Commit history","id":"history","x":"Commit Date Message 5df1efa 2026-10-24 docs: add unified ACL-style report with bootstrap confidence intervals c83d51e 2026-10-24 feat: add explicit correctness and hallucination rate to Task 2 b205d73 2026-10-24 feat: finalize Task 1 report and run real Task 2 evaluation 2e87b8b 2026-10-23 feat: verify Task 2 setup and mock metrics 4fa0cde 2026-10-23 feat: plot evaluation quality and latency 965bb5c 2026-10-23 feat: prepare reproducible Telugu test datasets 02c63c6 2026-10-23 docs: record incremental commit workflow 56fcb35 2026-10-23 feat: add paired speech pipeline evaluation e3dfd04 2026-10-23 feat: add Telugu speech pipeline Check the repository for anything newer than this table."},{"p":"repro","t":"Reproducibility","h":"Rebuilding the PDFs","id":"paper","x":"cd reports pdflatex report.tex # twice, for cross-references pdflatex report.tex pdflatex task1.tex pdflatex task2.tex The paper's Part II figures include outputs/plots/*.png ; task2.tex does too. task1.tex is self-contained (its diagrams are TikZ). The sources contain no Telugu script because plain pdflatex cannot render it."},{"p":"requirements","t":"Requirements","h":"Requirements","id":"","x":"Start Requirements What the report is required to cover, and a checklist mapping each requirement to the page that meets it."},{"p":"requirements","t":"Requirements","h":"Scope","id":"scope","x":"The report has two parts. Part I is an analysis of speech language-model architectures with a proposed improvement. Part II is an implemented, evaluated Indic speech pipeline. The focus throughout is research reasoning: identifying gaps, forming hypotheses, designing experiments, and reporting results honestly, rather than only implementing existing models. Area Speech foundation models, Speech LLMs, ASR, multilingual (Indic) speech understanding Part I Comparative architectural analysis of two recent Speech LLMs, with diagrams and a proposed improvement Part II A reproducible Indic speech pipeline with its own evaluation infrastructure, evaluated on at least 100 examples Deliverables A PDF "},{"p":"requirements","t":"Requirements","h":"Part I: architecture analysis","id":"part1","x":"Select and critically analyze two recent Speech LLM architectures, at least one with speech-to-speech generation. Deliver a comparative analysis with diagrams and a proposed improvement. Requirement Where it is met Status Two recent Speech LLMs Moshi and Qwen2.5-Omni. Why these two Met At least one speech-to-speech Both generate speech: Moshi end to end, Qwen2.5-Omni via its Talker Met 1. Speech encoder and representation learning Axis 1 Met 2. Semantic vs. acoustic representations Axis 2 : Moshi's split quantizer, Qwen's Thinker/Talker conditioning Met 3. Speech-to-LLM alignment (adapters, projectors, cross-attention) Axis 3 Met 4. Streaming vs. non-streaming Axis 4 Met 5. Autoregressive vs"},{"p":"requirements","t":"Requirements","h":"Part II: implementation and evaluation","id":"part2","x":"Build an Indic pipeline (speech input, ASR, language model, text response) from pretrained models, with the evaluation infrastructure written in the repository, evaluated on at least 100 examples. Requirement Where it is met Status Indic speech-to-text-response pipeline Pipeline : Telugu Whisper fine-tune to Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct Met Own evaluation infrastructure evaluate.py (Levenshtein WER and CER from scratch), prepare_data.py , plot_results.py , hallucination.py , bootstrap.py . No evaluation library is used. Modules Met At least 100 examples 129 per condition. Dataset Met ASR WER and CER Corpus and per-sample, with mean, median and p95. Results Met Response correctness and hallucination"},{"p":"requirements","t":"Requirements","h":"Deliverables","id":"deliverables","x":"Item Location Single PDF report covering both parts report.pdf Longer standalone write-ups task1.pdf , task2.pdf Code, configs, tests and README with reproduction steps GitHub repository ; see also Quickstart"},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"Results","id":"","x":"Part II: Pipeline Results Three real evaluation runs of 129 samples each, on a laptop GPU, with the models and metrics described in the previous pages. Every number here comes from the saved report.json files. Setup in one line vasista22/whisper-telugu-small to Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct , fp16, greedy decoding, RTX 4060 Laptop, transformers 4.57.6. Conditions: Clean ; Noisy (0 dB SNR, no denoising); Noisy+DN (same noisy audio, spectral-subtraction denoising on). 129 of 129 samples succeeded in every run."},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"Main results","id":"baseline","x":"Metric Clean Noisy Noisy+DN Corpus WER 0.2928 0.3377 0.3391 Corpus CER 0.0745 0.0964 0.0992 Pipeline RTF (corpus) 4.154 4.993 4.948 ASR-only RTF (corpus) 0.501 0.587 0.579 Mean ASR time (s) 1.742 2.054 2.026 Mean LLM time (s) 12.712 15.417 15.285 Mean total time (s) 14.455 17.471 17.311 Response F1, answer from ASR transcript 0.1657 0.1548 0.1547 Response F1, answer from human transcript 0.1687 0.1687 0.1687 Mean quality drop (human minus ASR) 0.0031 0.0139 0.0141 Samples degraded / improved by ASR errors 52.7% / 37.2% 55.0% / 34.9% 56.6% / 34.9% Exact-match rate (either arm) 0 0 0 Samples succeeded / failed 129 / 0 129 / 0 129 / 0 The human-transcript F1 is the same in every column because "},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"Distributions","id":"distributions","x":"Metric Statistic Clean Noisy Noisy+DN Per-sample WER mean 0.317 0.360 0.362 p50 0.333 0.333 0.333 p95 0.667 0.667 0.750 Per-sample CER mean 0.079 0.105 0.106 p50 0.053 0.083 0.077 p95 0.215 0.310 0.326 ASR time (s) mean 1.742 2.054 2.026 p50 1.531 2.142 2.114 p95 2.581 2.705 2.605 LLM time (s) mean 12.712 15.417 15.285 p50 10.893 15.763 15.608 p95 17.839 17.317 17.016 Total time (s) mean 14.455 17.471 17.311 p50 12.122 17.675 17.436 p95 20.236 19.489 19.210 Per-sample RTF mean 4.252 5.190 5.146 p50 4.425 4.969 4.962 p95 5.830 7.682 7.463 The per-sample WER median is a coarse 0.333 because the questions are short (a few words), so a single word error moves the rate in large steps. That is als"},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"Latency: where the time goes","id":"latency","x":"The ASR stage is real time : ASR-only RTF is 0.50 to 0.59, so it transcribes faster than the audio plays. The full pipeline is not : RTF is 4.15 to 4.99, because the LLM stage takes about 88% of pipeline time (12.71 of 14.45 s clean; 15.42 of 17.47 s noisy). Noisy runs are slower: mean LLM time rises by about 2.7 s. With greedy decoding on a fixed token budget this is consistent with noisier transcripts producing longer or different generations, not with any change to the LLM. Scope of the latency claim The 88% share is a property of this setup: a 1.5B model on a laptop GPU, greedy decoding, up to 256 new tokens. It is not a statement about cascades in general. These are also not the streami"},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"Correctness and hallucination","id":"correctness","x":"From the key-fact classifier , out of 129 answers per arm: Condition Answer from Correct Abstained Hallucinated Hallucination rate Clean ASR transcript 0 0 129 1.000 Clean human transcript 3 0 126 0.977 Noisy ASR transcript 0 0 129 1.000 Noisy human transcript 3 0 126 0.977 Noisy+DN ASR transcript 0 0 129 1.000 Noisy+DN human transcript 3 0 126 0.977 The LLM is the failure, and it never abstains Even from a perfect transcript, only 3 of 129 answers state the right fact. Across all six arms (774 answers) the model abstained zero times, so every miss is a confident non-answer or wrong answer. Typically it restates the question in fluent, repetitive Telugu without naming the capital or the sum."},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"Do the differences exceed noise?","id":"ci","x":"95% paired bootstrap intervals (10,000 resamples over the 129 questions, paired by question): Comparison Metric Difference 95% CI Reading Clean to Noisy WER +0.0449 +0.0141 to +0.0769 Real increase Clean to Noisy CER +0.0219 +0.0083 to +0.0364 Real increase Noisy to Noisy+DN WER +0.0014 -0.0144 to +0.0174 Indistinguishable from 0 Noisy to Noisy+DN CER +0.0028 -0.0041 to +0.0099 Indistinguishable from 0 Noise genuinely hurts recognition (the interval excludes zero even though the clean and noisy renderings differ). Denoising does not measurably change it. See the extension page ."},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"Plots","id":"plots","x":"Each condition has a WER-vs-quality scatter (ASR-transcript and human-transcript series share the same WER x-value per sample) and a stacked latency bar. WER is a ratio and is not clipped at 1. Clean: per-sample WER against response F1. Clean: mean latency; the LLM stage dominates. Noisy (0 dB), no denoising: per-sample WER against response F1. Noisy (0 dB), no denoising: mean latency. Noisy + denoising: per-sample WER against response F1. Noisy + denoising: mean latency."},{"p":"results","t":"Results","h":"What the results establish","id":"summary","x":"Claim Evidence The pipeline works end to end on 129 Telugu utterances 129/129 succeeded in all three runs Telugu ASR is usable but imperfect on clean synthetic speech WER 0.293, CER 0.075 Noise measurably hurts ASR WER +0.045, CI excludes 0 Spectral-subtraction denoising does not fix it WER +0.001, CI -0.014 to +0.017 The LLM, not ASR, limits answer quality 0/129 correct from speech, 3/129 from perfect transcripts, 0 abstentions The cascade is not real time end to end here RTF 4.15 to 4.99; LLM about 88% of time What they do not establish is covered on Limitations : synthetic speech, small models, a lexical quality proxy, and a single deterministic run per condition."},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"Architecture analysis","id":"","x":"Part I: Analysis Architecture analysis Two recent speech LLMs, compared on seven architectural axes, and an answer to the research question: what architectural limitations stop speech LLMs from low-latency, natural, real-time conversation? No experiments in this part Everything about the models here is a statement from the two source reports, cited by section and printed page (M = Moshi, Q = Qwen2.5-Omni). Consequences drawn from them are interpretation, and the proposal is a proposal. No gain, dataset size, parameter count or runtime is claimed."},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"The thesis","id":"thesis","x":"Streaming speech generation is not sufficient for natural real-time conversation , because conversational control, deciding when to continue, hold, or yield while still listening, is not fully specified as a separate policy in these reports and is not measured by first-audio latency. This is about control of the conversational floor , not about emitting audio incrementally. Moshi explicitly supports simultaneous listening and speaking, with turn-taking learned inside joint stream modeling. Qwen2.5-Omni describes streaming perception and generation. Those are different kinds of evidence. The research opportunity is to test whether a separately supervised controller can better tell a listener'"},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"Why these two models","id":"choice","x":"Moshi (Défossez et al., 2024): a full-duplex speech-text foundation model for real-time dialogue. Speech to speech. Qwen2.5-Omni (Qwen Team, 2025): a streaming omni-modal model with a Thinker (text LLM) and a Talker (speech generator). Also produces speech. Both are recent, both stream, and both produce speech, so the \"at least one speech-to-speech\" requirement is met twice. They also represent two different answers to the same problem: one jointly models both speakers' audio streams, the other bolts a speech decoder onto a multimodal LLM."},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"Seven-axis comparison","id":"axes","x":"Each cell is a statement from the cited report. Section and page references use the report's printed page numbers. Axis Moshi Qwen2.5-Omni 1. Encoder and representation learning Mimi: causal SeaNet convolutional codec (strides 4, 5, 6, 8, plus a final stride 2) with 8-layer causal Transformers before and after quantization. 24 kHz audio to 512-d at 12.5 Hz. 8 codebooks of 2,048 entries, 1.1 kbps. (M §3.3.1, pp.10-11) 128-channel mel input (25 ms window, 10 ms hop), about 40 ms per representation. Audio encoder initialized from Whisper-large-v3, with block-wise attention. (Q §2.2, p.4; §3, p.6) 2. Semantic vs. acoustic representations Split quantizer: one semantic VQ distilled from WavLM (cos"},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"What the reports do not specify","id":"unreported","x":"These gaps block matched comparison. They do not show a performance failure. Neither report fully specifies input-endpoint, playback-completion/flush, or session-stop rules. Qwen2.5-Omni leaves interruption handling unspecified. Qwen2.5-Omni does not report output-codec rate, codebooks or bitrate; adapter dimensions or depth; output-block duration; numerical end-to-end latency; audio training hours or named corpora; or complete tokenizer objectives. Moshi's practical-latency figure comes without a complete measurement protocol. Qwen's \"causal decoder\" wording sits beside a DiT with one lookahead block. Per-stage compute durations (encoder, Transformer, decoder, queue) are unreported for both"},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"Three ranked limitations","id":"limitations","x":"This is the answer to the research question. Each limitation is interpretation built on the cited mechanisms, not a result either paper reports."},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"L1. Continuous generation leaves conversational decisions implicit","id":"l1","x":"Moshi's joint streams remove the turn boundary and let it listen while speaking (M §1, p.2; §3.4.4, p.17). What remains open is when that learned behavior correctly keeps or gives up the floor. Moshi evaluates gaps, overlaps and dialogue quality (M §5.6, p.31), but aggregate distributions cannot show whether one backchannel caused an inappropriate stop. Continuous modeling is a prerequisite for studying floor control, not evidence that it is handled well. Qwen2.5-Omni's streaming does not establish event-level behavior either."},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"L2. Low transport delay does not establish timely social action","id":"l2","x":"Causal frames and hierarchical prediction (Moshi), or block-wise processing and bounded-context waveform decoding (Qwen), shorten the delay between input and output audio. But first-audio latency measures onset, not whether the system stops after a real interruption while talking through a backchannel. Two trivial strategies expose the problem. Suppressing output whenever speech appears gives fast stopping for free. Refusing to stop gives few false yields for free. Neither is a better conversation. Any evaluation must compare false yields only under common interruption-latency and missed-interruption limits, and the timing account has to include input buffering, controller computation and pl"},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"L3. Available cues and supervision may be insufficient","id":"l3","x":"Speech activity says that someone is making sound, not why. Telling acknowledgment, hesitation and interruption apart needs context, and generation state (is the assistant finishing a phrase, waiting, or mid-explanation?) is a candidate cue whose usefulness has to be tested. Supervision matters too: Moshi notes limits of simulated dialogue without overlap, and Qwen acknowledges noisy speech supervision (M §4.4, pp.20-21; Q §4.3, p.7). A measured gain must be attributed to a controlled intervention, not to differences between training corpora."},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"\"End of speech\" is four decisions","id":"four-decisions","x":"End-of-speech detection and stopping criteria are part of the analysis. The key observation is that the phrase conflates four decisions a conversational system must make separately. Decision Meaning Observable contrast Input endpoint The user has finished the current contribution Hesitation pause vs. completed question Conversational yield The assistant gives the user the floor Backchannel vs. genuine interruption Output completion The intended response has finished playing Intended end vs. playback and queue state Session termination Conversation processing stops running Explicit stop vs. ongoing listening Moshi establishes continuous listening and generation including silence; PAD/EPAD con"},{"p":"task1","t":"Architecture analysis","h":"Detailed evidence","id":"evidence","x":"Moshi: numbers and training stages Mimi encoder: four conv blocks with strides (4, 5, 6, 8) and a final stride-2 convolution; 24 kHz mono to 512-d at 12.5 frames/s. Transformer modules before and after quantization each have 8 layers and 8 heads, causal. (M §3.3.1, p.10) 8 codebooks x 2,048 entries = 1.1 kbps at 12.5 Hz. Split quantization: 1 semantic VQ + 7-level acoustic RVQ. (M §3.3.1-3.3.2, pp.11-12) WavLM teacher: 1,024-d at 50 Hz, pooled to 12.5 Hz; cosine distillation. (M §3.3.2, p.12) Joint sequence has K = 2Q + 1 streams with Q = 8; actual user audio replaces predicted user-stream tokens during interaction. (M §3.4.4, p.17) Acoustic delay 2 in pretraining, 1 in fine-tuning. (M §5.3,"},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Tests","id":"","x":"Reference Tests 30 unit tests in 8 files, all offline: no model downloads, no GPU, no network. They protect the evaluation code, which is where a silent bug would quietly corrupt every reported number. python -m unittest discover -s tests -v Expected: Ran 30 tests and OK in about two seconds. Use -s tests : the folder is not a package, so python -m unittest tests.test_x will not import. What passing means It means the metric arithmetic, dataset handling, config guards, plotting and the denoise, hallucination and bootstrap logic behave as specified on constructed inputs. It does not mean the models are accurate; model quality is measured by the evaluation, not by the tests."},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Coverage by file","id":"summary","x":"File Tests Covers test_evaluate.py 5 Edit distance and Unicode policy; quality score and RTF; dataset loading and validation; paired comparison and corpus weighting; batch reporting and failure handling test_pipeline.py 4 Stage hand-off and the empty-transcript guard; config paths and validation; audio mono mix, resampling and missing file; LLM returns only the continuation and enforces the input budget test_prepare_data.py 3 Folder round trip and seeded subset reproducibility; manifest column mapping and explicit placeholders; invalid sources and duplicate audio test_plot_results.py 3 JSON and CSV round trip; missing scatter pairs and optional latency; invalid data is not silently plotted t"},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Notable details","id":"details","x":""},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Metrics are checked against hand-computed values","id":"metrics-tests","x":"test_evaluate.py and the self-check inside verify_task2.py use fixed cases with known answers (perfect match, substitution, deletion, insertion, empty reference, Unicode normalization), so a regression in the edit-distance code shows up as a mismatch rather than a plausible-looking number."},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Models are replaced by stand-ins","id":"no-models","x":"Pipeline and evaluation tests inject simple fake ASR and LLM objects, and the verifier test patches from_pretrained to fail if a download is attempted, so they run anywhere. The wrappers' audio handling is tested on tiny generated WAV files."},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Bad input must fail loudly","id":"bad-input","x":"Several tests assert errors rather than success: malformed datasets, duplicate IDs, missing audio, wrong-shaped plot data, a wrong metric value in the verifier, an out-of-range denoise parameter, a mismatched bootstrap pair. The project's rule is that invalid data should never be silently dropped or plotted."},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"A test that was wrong first","id":"denoise-test","x":"The first denoise test used a constant sine tone, and failed (error went up, not down). The cause was the algorithm's assumption that each frequency bin has quiet frames, which a sustained tone does not. The test was corrected to use a bursty tone with gaps, which resembles speech, rather than weakening the assertion. See the extension page ."},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Growth of the suite","id":"history","x":"Stage Tests Original suite (pipeline, evaluate, prepare, plot, verify) 20 + denoise 23 + hallucination classifier 27 + bootstrap 30"},{"p":"tests","t":"Tests","h":"Not covered by tests","id":"not-covered","x":"Real model inference (that is the evaluation itself). synthesize_corpus.py ' TTS step, which needs a model download. Its pure helpers ( build_qa_pairs , add_noise ) are not unit-tested either. The LaTeX reports and this documentation site."}];
